# Files currently over the size limit, frozen at their present length.
#
# Scope is verify_code_size.py's, not "shell": linux/scripts, linux/host-config and
# docs/scripts by *.sh/*.py, plus the Dockerfile* at the top of linux/.
#
# Format: <path> | <lines> | <reason>
#
# Same four-way contract as function-size.allow: growth, a new offender, an
# unrecorded shrink and a stale entry all fail. Shrinking a file means updating
# its number here; deleting the line once it drops under the limit.
#
# The refactor entries live in docs/refactoring-backlog.md F2.
docs/scripts/bump_versions.py | 1121 | baseline 2026-09-03: newly in scope (the scan gained *.py and Dockerfile*); F2 reviewed 2026-09-04, NOT a split target: one subject, the versions.env updater. ~370 of the 1121 lines are the spec_* table — one 5-15 line function per tracked upstream, every one of them calling the http_*/gh_*/asset_sha256 helpers defined above it. Moving the table to a sibling buys a two-way import and no comprehension: the length here is the NUMBER OF UPSTREAMS, not tangled logic. Outside the build closure, so it can be cut any day the count doubles
docs/scripts/sync_versions.py | 883 | 882 -> 883 on 2026-09-07: the module docstring was claiming SIX consumers while --check runs seven -- check_doc_literals had no line, and "shell-script defaults" named PowerShell -DefaultValue pins. +1 line for the two it was missing. 873 -> 882 on 2026-09-07: F3's two-syntax overlap got one owner -- _rewrite_lines (the newline='' round trip both syncers share) plus _unquote, with each syncer reduced to its own per-line decision. Net +9 lines here because the shared comment about frozen per-file EOLs is now written once, properly, instead of twice in shorthand. baseline 2026-09-03: newly in scope (the scan gained *.py and Dockerfile*); F2 reviewed 2026-09-04, NOT a split target: one subject — propagate versions.env into every file that repeats a version — over six targets (inline markers, snapshot block, deps table, Dockerfile ARGs, PowerShell script defaults, doc literals), each a check_X/write_X pair over the same parse_versions_env/collect_versions. Six ~120-line files importing the same three helpers reads worse than one, and it is only 49 lines over. F2 2026-09-05: 849 -> 873, +24 for the module docstring this file had never had (shebang straight into `from __future__`, unlike its sibling bump_versions.py) -- it now states the six consumers, why --write does the Dockerfiles first, and that a malformed marker fails both modes. Growth that BUYS the reader the map; the not-a-split verdict above is unchanged
linux/Dockerfile.media | 1162 | baseline 2026-09-03: newly in scope (the scan gained *.py and Dockerfile*); F2 reviewed 2026-09-04, NOT splittable at all — a Dockerfile has no include mechanism (the same fact that closed the RUN-mount-preamble clone family as not-actionable), and the length IS the media stage chain: ffmpeg, gstreamer, opencv, onnxruntime, litert, libcamera, iree. Splitting it means splitting the stage graph, i.e. a different build. This row can only ever catch growth; that is what it is for
linux/scripts/02-toolchain/build-gcc.sh | 881 | F2 reviewed 2026-09-04, NOT a split target: one stage's script for one subject — fetch, verify, configure, build, register GCC. The seams that could be named already are (verify_gcc_gpg_signature, verify_gcc_sha512, fetch_gcc_tarball, cache_store_gcc_tarball, finish_libtool_dirs); what is left is usage() plus a ~40-flag CLI parser plus one straight-line procedure, and a procedure is not two subjects. Inside the build closure, so a split would need a chain to prove it — and would buy nothing. +1 on 2026-09-05: YB's compiler_cache_launcher_env at the launcher resolution site -- the sccache server address has to be established in the shell that runs the compiles
linux/scripts/03-media/build/litert/build-litert.sh | 953 | F2 reviewed 2026-09-04, NOT a split target: one stage, one subject (build LiteRT + its wheel, per arch), and the one genuinely separable piece — the QAIRT download guard — was already moved out to android/litert-qairt-guard.sh, which is where the -22 came from. Inside the build closure. HISTORY 2026-09-03: +_litert_qairt_include_dir, then -22 as the QAIRT guard moved to android/litert-qairt-guard.sh (YA)
linux/scripts/03-media/build/opencv/build-opencv.sh | 955 | 936 -> 955 on 2026-09-07: F1's named seams cut. _ota_riscv64_freetype and _ota_riscv64_png are now their own functions with their own headers, which is +19 lines here and takes _opencv_target_adjustments under BOTH limits (114 -> under 80 lines, cc 33 -> under 15) -- both allow rows deleted rather than re-baselined. F2 reviewed 2026-09-04, NOT a split target: one stage, one subject; the bulk is _opencv_target_adjustments and the cmake argument assembly, which are the per-arch knowledge this file exists to hold — splitting them apart puts the flag and the reason for the flag in different files. Inside the build closure. HISTORY: baseline 2026-09-03; +1 on 2026-09-04 for the `: "${OPENCV_GSTREAMER_PASS:=1}"` self-default that let the knob's registry row be deleted. +1 on 2026-09-05: YB's compiler_cache_launcher_env at the launcher resolution site -- the sccache server address has to be established in the shell that runs the compiles
linux/scripts/05-frameworks/torch/build-app-wheelhouse.sh | 1248 | F2 reviewed 2026-09-04, the ONE row where the two-subjects test nearly bites: torch/vision wheel resolution and staging is one subject, build_iree_wheels is another, and they share only the wheelhouse directory. NOT split now because it is 05-frameworks, inside the build closure, and no chain in this session validates a change there. The suite is NOT a blocker: test-iree-wheelhouse-stages.sh (the only file referencing it) extracts by awk PATTERN range anchored on the function heads and asserts the block contains them, so a split that moved them would fail it loudly rather than re-aim silently. Revisit in a window where a chain can prove it. HISTORY: baseline 2026-09-03; +3 on 2026-09-04 for the IREE_CROSS_BUILD_COMPILER self-default inside build_iree_wheels (the stage suites extract that block alone, so a file-scope default is invisible to it) plus its two-line reason. +2 on 2026-09-05: YB's compiler_cache_launcher_env at the launcher resolution sites (target build + IREE) -- the sccache server address has to be established in the shell that runs the compiles
linux/scripts/06-packaging/smoke-runtime-image.sh | 2433 | 2387 -> 2433 on 2026-09-07: R1.1, the runtime-side ldd walk over /usr/local/llvm-target/bin -- the sdk stage checks NEEDED against the BUILDER's ldconfig cache, which is how liblldb shipped 3 unstartable binaries of amd64's 142. 2336 -> 2387 on 2026-09-07: VK3's toolset ratchet -- the twenty required tool names, the per-arch frozen floors (_VK_TOOLSET_FROZEN) and _vk_floor_verdict, which is the assertion that 2-of-52 could never have survived. F2 reviewed 2026-09-04 — the backlog names this as the one to split if any gets split; the review says NO. 91 functions -- 45 check_*/_probe_* assertions plus the probe-and-verdict layer they share -- over ONE image through ONE _rt_run, driven by one main(). (This row said "63 functions, all of them check_*/_probe_*" until 2026-09-07; it was never true.) Its length is the NUMBER OF ASSERTIONS about the shipped bytes, and that number growing is the gate succeeding, not debt. The only real seam is the shipped-truth payload probe -- _shipped_truth_probe / run_shipped_truth_probe and the verdict functions reading their output, named rather than line-ranged because the old ~923-1386 was ~600 lines stale by 2026-09-07 -- which collects once and derives verdicts, because a per-check exec costs a container start — that is one subject with two probe strategies, not two subjects. Splitting it would mean the file a reader opens to answer "what do we actually assert about the shipped image?" is no longer one file. HISTORY: grew 2026-09-04: HT1 check_manifest_tree_arch — the manifest-driven ELF-machine audit that generalises check_flutter/check_rust_toolchain (docs/artifact-copy-completeness.md#the-shipped-trees-must-carry-the-images-own-arch); 2026-09-04: the HT1 tree-arch scanner reads candidates (executable/.so) before the rest and a capped walk is now a FAIL, not an INFO line; 2026-09-04: check_consumer_contract — the seven-row contract a consuming CI lane depends on and cannot repair from inside a read-only overlay layer, one probe plus five pure verdict functions (docs/consumer-image-contract.md#the-contract); 2026-09-04: the consumer-contract gate gained its JDK row (probe facts, verdict, symptom); +24 for the appimagetool contract row (probe fact, verdict, symptom); +47 on 2026-09-05: the cross-payload rule the tree-arch scanner needed, and the frozen-finding table (HT2); +2 on 2026-09-05 (lane llvm-host-libs) for emptying _RT_TREE_ARCH_FROZEN and pointing it at the doc -- the five llvm-target x86-64 libs it froze are fixed at the source now (HT3); +16 on 2026-09-05 (lane ht5-vulkan) for check_vulkan_loader reading which libvulkan actually answered and the whole-tree Vulkan probe (docs/artifact-copy-completeness.md#the-vulkan-tree-ships-only-what-the-image-runs); +47 on 2026-09-05 for check_vulkan_toolset -- the loader check proved libvulkan answers, nothing asserted the prefix carries tools you can build a shader with, and a library-only prefix shipped for months (docs/vulkan-foreign-arch-sdk.md); +104 on 2026-09-05 for check_android_abi -- /opt/android is tree-arch EXEMPT because an Android payload is never the image's own arch, so nothing could see that the ABI derived from the BUILD HOST and shipped x86_64 to every image; it reads .a members too, because the object that broke a consumer's link sat inside an archive (docs/linux-cross-builds.md#the-android-abi-is-a-target-not-the-build-host); +4 on 2026-09-05 to advertise and check WASM_PACK_VERSION and FLUTTER_RUST_BRIDGE_VERSION -- the ADV/HAVE row is also the proof that the two web-lane binaries are installed at their pins; +34 on 2026-09-05 for three contract rows that ask whether a thing is STAGED rather than writable (flatpak runtimes, the AppImage runtime, the two web-lane binaries) -- one shared verdict, and their cost stays written down once in _consumer_contract_symptom
linux/scripts/build-cross-chain.sh | 912 | 890 -> 912 on 2026-09-07: DISK3's two call sites plus _chain_evict_slugs and the two measure functions it takes -- the extraction is +21 lines here and -9 cc in the guard, which is the trade F1 asked for. F2 reviewed 2026-09-04, NOT a split target: it is the orchestrator, and its subject is exactly the thing that must be read in one place — stage order, lifecycle, the EXIT trap and the disk guard. Its extractable helpers already live in 01-core (cross-stage-build.sh, disk-guard.sh, stage-defs.sh, chain-verify.sh); what remains is the sequencing that calls them. Inside the build closure. HISTORY: baseline 2026-09-03; +1 on 2026-09-04 turning _chain_on_exit's trailing `&&` list into an `if` — an EXIT trap ending on a false && returns 1 and fails a chain that finished green; 2026-09-04: DISK1: the in-stage sampler reaches for the filtered buildkit reclaim; +7 on 2026-09-05 wiring that reclaim into the two gates that own an episode — _disk_guard_reclaim_begin before each eviction pass, and a _disk_guard_buildkit_fallback plus its free-space re-read once threshold pruning falls short
linux/scripts/tests/test-runtime-image-gates.sh | 1086 | 1048 -> 1086 on 2026-09-07: the llvm-target startability cases -- clean, the liblldb shape, a probe that did not run, an absent prefix, and the call site in main(). 1005 -> 1048 on 2026-09-07: the five frozen-floor cases -- a prefix that keeps every required NAME can still lose tools, which is what 2-of-52 looked like, so the floors get their own below/at/above cases plus the amd64 row and the no-row arch; 985 -> 1005 on 2026-09-07: the verdict-vocabulary case -- baseline 2026-09-05: the suite that proves smoke-runtime-image.sh's gates can fail, and its length is the same number its subject's row is — one _extract of the real function plus stubs per assertion, 76 t_cases over seven gate families (WE/WF healthcheck and wheel ratchet, XQ default boot, advertised keys, HT1 tree-arch and its host-side halves, the consumer contract). Splitting by family is possible but buys nothing: the _extract harness and its stub table are shared by all of them, so seven files would each re-source it and the reader loses the one place that answers "what makes these gates bite?". 767 -> 837 on 2026-09-05 for the cross-payload arm of the tree-arch gate; 837 -> 872 on 2026-09-05 (lane ht5-vulkan) for the four Vulkan-loader verdicts and the un-narrowed tree probe; 872 -> 917 on 2026-09-05 for the seven check_vulkan_toolset cases -- the gate that fails on the shape that actually shipped (a target SDK prefix with full libraries and two binaries), with its required/reported lists read out of the source so the suite cannot drift from it (docs/vulkan-foreign-arch-sdk.md); 917 -> 966 on 2026-09-05 for the seven check_android_abi cases, including the mixed-payload one -- one stale ABI among many is how the wrong objects actually reached a consumer, and the shipped/fixed fixtures are hoisted so the two verdicts about the same payload are not two copies of it; +18 on 2026-09-06 for the exemption-handoff case -- /opt/android leaves tree-arch and check_android_abi takes it over, which is only honest while that gate actually walks the tree
linux/scripts/02-toolchain/vulkan.sh | 883 | 809 -> 883 on 2026-09-07: VK2's four remaining components -- the two config-package rows, _vulkan_target_dynamic_args (the Canadian-cross paths a static table cannot carry) and the required-component verdict with its _vk_note_failure owner. baseline 2026-09-05: one subject -- provision the Vulkan SDK for the host AND cross-build the target-arch prefix the image runs. The two halves share the SDK layout, the checkout names and the pkg-config environment that has to differ between them, which is exactly the bug that cost the sdk stage a run; splitting them would put that shared contract in two files. Grew past 800 when the target build stopped being four components and became the whole SDK (docs/vulkan-foreign-arch-sdk.md)

# --- EX1 2026-09-07: linux/llm-stack enters the scan set. These 47 rows are the
# territory the extent gates could never see; every one was read and given a
# verdict in the same wave that widened SCAN, so none is a bare baseline. 34 of
# them say DEBT and name their seam -- that is the honest state of a benchmark
# harness nobody had reviewed for shape. docs/refactoring-backlog.md EX1
linux/llm-stack/bench_agent.py | 1089 | SPLIT TARGET: YES. 356 of the 1089 lines are inert fixture literals — TASKS 233 lines (245-477), REFERENCE 86 (485-570), CHEATS 22 (575-596), CLAMP_MUTANTS 15 (150-164) — i.e. the source files of five scratch repositories (calc.py, utils.py, client/report, list-files.sh + check.sh, CMakeLists.txt + mathlib.c + test_math.c), their reference solutions and their cheat variants, embedded as Python strings; the remaining 591 lines are 26 functions averaging 23 lines, and only main exceeds the 80-line function limit. The seam is that corpus plus the task-bound checkers it points at — check_clamp_tests_kill_mutants, check_rename_complete and old_name_uses — into bench_agent_fixtures.py (~490 lines), leaving the opencode driver (run_agent, agent_errors, summarise_events, verify, run_task, main) at ~600; corpus and checkers must move together because each TASKS entry's "checks" key holds those function objects, so moving the data alone would make the fixtures module import back into the driver. The fixtures must stay strings rather than become real files on disk: fix_bash_quoting's list-files.sh is deliberately written with an unquoted `for f in $@` and would trip this repo's own shellcheck/lint-shell gate the moment EX1 widens the scan into linux/llm-stack. Nothing else blocks the split — tests/test_bench_agent.py (1011 lines) reaches every one of these through `import bench_agent as ba`, so the move is a rename away from being covered.
linux/llm-stack/bench_coding.py | 2053 | 2053 raw lines that break down as 196 blank, 204 comment-only, 268 docstring and 409 lines of top-level literal (TASKS 56-202 = 147, NOVEL_TASKS 230-380 = 151, _BASH_PRELUDE/_CMAKE_PRELUDE/_CMAKE_REPORT/_DOCKERFILE_PREAMBLE 1129-1244 = 111), which still leaves ~976 lines of executable code -- over the 800 limit with every line of prose and data already removed, so unlike bench_tasks.py this is not a data file that happens to be long. SPLIT TARGET: YES, and the seam is run_candidate at line 1015. Everything from CODE_FENCE (388) to _RUNNERS (1432) is one subject -- turn an untrusted reply into a verdict: extract_code/_defines/_is_preamble, looks_truncated/_unclosed_fence, the forbidden-name AST stack (_scope_nodes/_bound_names/_is_lookup_string/_unbound_uses/check_forbidden/_check_forbidden_text/_nonstd_imports), the sandbox (_netns_available/_nproc_ceiling/_candidate_rlimits/_communicate_bounded/_kill_tree/_launch), _assertion_harness, and the four runners with their embedded bash/CMake/Dockerfile preludes. Everything from ask (1437) to main (2049) is a different subject: drive an endpoint, time it, classify the attempt, rank the models. The two halves touch at exactly four names -- run_candidate, extract_code, looks_truncated, generation_cap -- so a bench_grader.py import is one-way with no cycle, and the task literals can follow the precedent bench_tasks.py already set for EXTENDED_TASKS/LANGUAGE_TASKS. Nothing blocks it: 865 tests live in linux/llm-stack/tests, 33 assertions call extract_code and 27 call looks_truncated, and docs/scripts/mutations.json carries 75 rows against this file -- more than any other file in the repo, including smoke-runtime-image.sh's 55 -- so the behaviour is pinned; the only edit beyond the move is the import line in six test modules.
linux/llm-stack/bench_tasks.py | 1889 | 1889 lines of which 1824 touch a string literal and only 65 are Python: ast.parse returns exactly three top-level nodes -- the docstring, EXTENDED_TASKS = [...] (21 tasks, lines 21-1557) and LANGUAGE_TASKS = [...] (6 tasks, 1559-1888) -- and there is no FunctionDef, AsyncFunctionDef or ClassDef anywhere in the tree, which is the whole reason this file carries no function-size and no cc/nesting row: verify_code_size._py_functions and verify_code_complexity's PY_DEF walk the same three node types and never enter anything here. The 24 lines beginning `def ` are all inside triple-quoted strings -- the per-task `reference` and `wrong` answer keys, including the nested `def num(s)` with six raise paths inside validation_parse_range_spec -- payload this module never executes (bench_coding.run_candidate does, in a subprocess after extract_code), so their absence from the complexity gate is the metric being literal, not the code being simple. Per key the 1824 payload lines are tests 584, prompt 449, reference 377, wrong 280, plus 27 each of name/kind/lang/wrong_explanation: four parallel texts per task, in python, bash, cmake and dockerfile. The length is therefore the NUMBER OF BENCHMARK TASKS (27) times the size of one fully specified task (39-126 lines, validation_parse_range_spec the largest at 126), and the 27 is itself the measurement decision the docstring records -- at n=6 the smallest provable drop is 100% -> 17%, so the coding column separated nobody, and tasks were added until it could. NOT a split target: the only seam that exists is already drawn, since LANGUAGE_TASKS is a second name that bench_coding.py:218 imports separately and `--task-set languages` selects on its own, and moving it to a sibling leaves 1557 lines here -- still nearly twice the 800 limit -- while both lists share one identical 8-key schema and one consumer. Splitting the 21 by family prefix (strings_/lists_/dicts_/validation_/numbers_/stateful_/parsing_) yields seven files that import nothing, export one list each, and force bench_coding.py plus test_bench_coding_tasks.py:138's ALL_TASKS to re-import all seven: a seam with no code on either side of it. Coverage is not what blocks a split -- every row is parametrized in tests/test_bench_coding_tasks.py as test_reference_solution_passes and test_wrong_solution_is_rejected plus TestTaskShape, so the schema is enforced -- there is simply nothing to extract. Frozen at 1889 the row can only catch growth, and a 28th task failing this gate until the number is re-baselined is correct: adding one changes what the benchmark measures and must be a reviewable edit.
linux/llm-stack/bench_tools.py | 1151 | baseline 2026-09-07, newly in scope: EX1 widens the extent gates to linux/llm-stack, which verify_code_size.py's SCAN (linux/scripts, linux/host-config, docs/scripts) has never covered. SPLIT TARGET: YES, and the seam is already a one-way boundary in the current file. The benchmark DEFINITION is ~590 lines -- TOOLS (8 schemas, 43-104), TOOL_SETS/TOOL_SET_SOURCES, CASES (33 cases over call/selection/arguments/typed_args/parallel/restraint/irrelevance, 115-257), MULTI_CASES (9 histories, 301-350) with its _DIFF/_tool_turn/_tool_turns/_long_test_output builders, and the pure grader layer JSON_TYPES..grade_error_recovery (460-792: 20 functions, no network, no state, its own 697-line tests/test_bench_tools.py) -- while the RUNNER is ~450: PAD_SOURCES/context_padding, call/call_multi, turn_growth, _message_hash, _translate_history, evaluate, _determinism_extra, main. Nothing in the definition half calls into the runner half (its only outward references are geniex_toolcall_shim.parse_tool_calls and tools_opencode), and tools_opencode.py already proves a corpus module lives beside this one and is selected through TOOL_SETS; the graders have to travel WITH the cases rather than into a third file, because the expect vocabulary is only defined by the pair -- {"contains": [...]} means nothing without _arg_matches, and the bare "README.md" in simple_read is why _norm_path's ./ leniency exists. Blast radius is small: bench_sweep.py:90 shells out to the file rather than importing it, so the only importers are the two test modules, and `from tools_cases import CASES, TOOLS` style re-exports keep `from bench_tools import CASES, TOOLS, grade` working unchanged. Two costs must be paid in the same commit: write_report's provenance tuple ("bench_tools.py", "tools_opencode.py", "bench_provenance.py") has to gain the new module or the report fingerprint silently stops covering the file that decides the score; and evaluate must keep reading module-level CASES/MULTI_CASES via `from ... import`, never `mod.CASES`, or tests/test_bench_tools_evaluate.py's monkeypatch.setattr(bt, "CASES", ...) stops taking effect and the suite goes vacuously green again -- exactly the failure R3 was written to end. Outside the build closure, so no chain is needed to prove any of it.
linux/llm-stack/benchmark_openai_api.py | 1049 | SPLIT TARGET: YES, and the module docstring names the seam itself — "It is also the de-facto library of the suite: the sibling tools import resolve_backend, load_backends and detect_model_via_api from here." Seven consumers do exactly that (bench_lanes.py:270 and :347, bench_sweep.py:118 and :269, bench_embeddings.py:180, bench_tools.py:1053, bench_coding.py:1942, bench_provenance.py:70, run_benchmarks.sh:38), so importing the backend registry drags in the prompt tables, the Glances sampler, benchmark_chat, print_table and main with it. The cut is precisely what the siblings import: BACKENDS_FILE/load_backends/resolve_backend/resolve_backend_entry/print_backends (167-267), _get_json/detect_model_via_api/resolve_model (392-441), and the correctness probe CORRECTNESS_PROBES/_answer_matches/run_correctness_probe (269-302, 628-722) — about 280 lines — into a bench_backends.py sitting beside bench_cli.py, which already owns the OTHER half of the same backends.json entry schema (api_key_env/headers/request_extra are interpreted here, while the headers they become are built by bench_cli.request_headers/entry_config/post_json). That leaves the runner at roughly 770 lines, under the 800 limit, so the split retires this row rather than relocating it. The one real obstacle is at 918-920: main() rebinds the module globals LLM_BASE_URL/OLLAMA_BASE_URL after resolution and benchmark_chat, run_correctness_probe and detect_model_via_api all read them as their base_url default, so the resolver cannot leave until main passes base_url explicitly at 933/938/980 (all three already take the parameter), and tests/test_backend_compat.py's subprocess assertion on b.LLM_BASE_URL moves with it. Outside the build closure — no chain is needed to prove any of this.
linux/llm-stack/tests/test_bench_agent.py | 1011 | baseline 2026-09-07: newly in scope (EX1 widened the extent scan to linux/llm-stack). The 1011 lines are 102 test functions across 17 classes -- every method under ten lines, which is why this file draws no function-size and no code-complexity row, only this one -- exercising 24 bench_agent symbols, and only 77 of the lines are multi-line fixture payload (45 comment, 177 blank). Each class is ONE scored defect and each method one way an agent number could have been wrong: D12 TestFixTaskProtectsItsTests (deleting, skipping or inverting the red test, deleting test_calc.py, or adding a conftest.py that rebinds calc.average, all of which used to print the same '2 passed' a real fix prints), D13 TestClampTaskRequiresRealTests (a correct clamp with test_utils.py untouched used to PASS; the cases walk vacuous / no-error-case / no-range / one-end-only and check_clamp_tests_kill_mutants, plus pytest's exit 5 not counting as a kill), D14 TestRenameTaskDecidesOnTheTree (a substring scan failed a correct rename over a comment; eleven cases separate a comment, a sentence docstring, a bare `"""fetch_data"""` and prefetch_database from an alias, a wrapper def, globals()['fetch_data'] and import-as, with old_name_uses falling back to text on an unparseable file), D15 TestErrorClassification (CONTEXT vs CONTEXT_GROWTH vs plain ERROR, and 'context canceled' never reading as an overflow), D17 TestTimeoutKeepsEvidence (partial events and stderr survive the kill and the grandchild PID is gone), D18 (a --task typo exits nonzero and writes no report bench_compare would read), R4 TestOpencodeProvenance/TestReportProvenance (load_jsonc, resolve_base_url, the config sha even when it does not parse, diff_sha256/DIFF_LIMIT truncation, the scratch XDG_DATA_HOME removed after the run) and R5 TestOtherLanguageFixtures (the bash and cmake fixtures, their `requires`, the make/ninja alternative group, and the '100% tests passed(, 0 tests failed)? out of 1' pattern that a zero-test ctest run can never print). SPLIT TARGET: YES. The seam is the fixture machinery and it already cuts the file in two with no class straddling it: TestFixtures, TestWorkspace, TestVerification, TestFixTaskProtectsItsTests, TestClampTaskRequiresRealTests, TestRenameTaskDecidesOnTheTree, TestOtherLanguageFixtures and TestProtectionIsNotVacuous (~455 lines) reach bench_agent only through solved_workspace/task_named/_skip_without_tools and ask 'does verify() refuse this cheat'; TestCli, TestErrorClassification, TestEventSummary, TestTimeoutKeepsEvidence, TestOpencodeProvenance, TestScoreExcludesBlockedRuns, TestReportProvenance, TestWriteReportExtra and TestSkipsAreVisible (~431 lines) reach it through run_main/fake_opencode or a subprocess and ask 'does a run produce an honest report'. Not one class uses a helper from the other family -- which is exactly the reason the comparable row on linux/scripts/tests/test-runtime-image-gates.sh says NOT a split target (there every family shares the one _extract harness and stub table), and that reason is absent here. Cut it into tests/test_bench_agent_verify.py + tests/test_bench_agent_run.py with task_named the only duplicated two lines (or a tests/conftest.py entry -- the directory already has a conftest for the offline guard); the halves land near 505 and 486 lines, under FILE_SIZE_LIMIT, so this row disappears rather than being re-baselined. What it costs: 29 entries in docs/scripts/mutations.json name this path with -k selectors (the bench-agent.* family plus bench-cli.extra-dropped and bench-cli.extra-incomplete-replaces) and must be re-aimed by hand -- but they cannot rot silently, because verify_mutations.passes() runs every command unmutated first, so a pytest exit 4 (path gone) or 5 (a -k that now matches nothing) fails loudly as 'baseline test already fails unmutated'. Nothing else in the tree names the file: CI runs the directory (.github/workflows/llm-stack-tests.yml: pytest linux/llm-stack/tests/ -v -m 'not inference'). Low priority -- outside the build closure, and every one of the 102 tests earns its lines -- but a real seam, not a count.
linux/llm-stack/tests/test_bench_coding_audit.py | 836 | 836 = 25 test classes and 73 test bodies, which expand to ~90 executed cases once the five parametrize sets do (4 fence spellings, 7 AST shapes, 6 tree evasions, 3 lookup evasions, 2 non-ASCII tasks), carrying 107 asserts across 584 code lines plus 120 lines of comment/docstring that state which audit reproduction each case pins; the file produces no function row and no cc/nesting row at all because its largest unit is a ~10-line test body and its only module-level machinery is _FakeResp (11 lines), _fake_ask_factory, _http_error and the MERGE/GOOD/STUB constants — so the number is the count of repaired defects, not tangle. It is still a SPLIT TARGET: YES, because the grouping is 'what the 2026-09-04 audit found', which is a provenance label, not a subject, and the per-test comments carry the provenance anyway, so the cut costs no narrative. The seam is three named pieces. (1) TestCompareUsesObservedCounts (lines 357-397) does not exercise bench_coding at all: it calls bench_compare.normalise/compare and reaches sideways with `import test_bench_compare as tbc` after sys.path.insert of its own directory, to borrow that suite's report_repeats builder — those two tests belong in test_bench_compare.py beside the builder they already depend on. (2) The eight accounting/transport classes that never call run_candidate and instead monkeypatch bc.ask or bc.urllib.request.urlopen and then read evaluate()'s ledger — TestAskReasoningDeltas, TestEvaluateAccounting, TestRealTokenCount, TestWallClockDeadline, TestInStreamErrors, TestContextOverflow, TestUnmeasuredWallTime — together with _FakeResp, _fake_ask_factory and _http_error, which no grader-side test uses, are ~300 lines and want their own test_bench_coding_accounting.py. (3) What remains (~530 lines: TestFences, TestHarnessCannotBeFakedCheaply, TestSetupStatements, TestAstGrouping, TestSubprocessHardening, the three Forbidden* classes, TestStdlibOnly, TestCandidateConfinement, TestCandidateRlimits, TestGraderSelfCheck) is the grader, which is the stated subject of the existing test_bench_coding.py. What blocks it today: GOOD/STUB/MERGE are used by both halves and there is no shared fixture home — tests/conftest.py is a network-policy file with an autouse no_network fixture and nothing else, and test_bench_coding.py already keeps a second private copy of MERGE/GOOD_MERGE — so step one is a fixture module those three files import rather than a fourth copy; and this pass is read-only, so the cut has to land in a window where pytest can prove the split green.
